│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ vectorize_balanced_packing                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ The main bottleneck is `balanced_packing`, which runs a pure-Python double loop over      │
│                          │ `num_layers * num_groups` items. This makes the algorithm itself slow (speed_score =      │
│                          │ 0.00), which drags down the combined score. We can batch the greedy LPT-style assignment  │
│                          │ across all layers: sort each layer's weights once (already done via torch), then iterate  │
│                          │ only over item positions (`num_groups` iterations). In each iteration, we pick, for every │
│                          │ layer at once, the pack with minimum accumulated weight among packs that still have       │
│                          │ capacity, and update the pack weights/counts with vectorized tensor ops. This reduces the │
│                          │ Python loop count by a factor of `num_layers` while keeping the exact same greedy         │
│                          │ assignment (descending order, least-loaded feasible pack), so balancedness is preserved   │
│                          │ and runtime drops substantially. The `groups_per_pack == 1` fast path is kept unchanged.  │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0045                                                                                   │
