│ patch_name               │ best_swap_local_search_refinement                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ GPU balancedness (0.66) is the dominant weakness. The existing `_refine_packing` only     │
│                          │ tries one swap per round (heaviest pack's smallest item ↔ lightest pack's largest item)   │
│                          │ and stops as soon as that single extreme pair fails, which gets stuck in poor local       │
│                          │ optima. I replace it with a stronger local search that, for the most-loaded pack's        │
│                          │ smallest item, evaluates the best partner pack among *all* other packs (each contributing │
│                          │ its largest item) and picks the swap that minimizes the resulting maximum pack load.      │
│                          │ Cardinality and rank-slot constraints are preserved exactly (swapped items inherit each   │
│                          │ other's pack and rank slots), so all downstream map construction (`inverse`, `gather`)    │
│                          │ still works verbatim. To keep it fast, the inner search runs on plain Python lists per    │
│                          │ layer with early termination when no improving swap exists, and per-pack item lists are   │
│                          │ maintained incrementally so each round costs O(n) instead of O(P·n) tensor ops. Since     │
│                          │ `balanced_packing` is used both for node packing (Step 1) and GPU packing (Step 3), the   │
│                          │ improved swap heuristic directly raises GPU-level balance without touching the risky      │
│                          │ hierarchical plumbing. More refinement rounds (64) are affordable because the loop breaks │
│                          │ as soon as convergence is reached.                                                        │
