                        Patch Metadata - Gen 22/100 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/3                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ waterfill_lpt_pairswap                                                                    │
│ patch_description        │ A fundamentally different two-stage approach. (1) Replication: instead of the sequential  │
│                          │ greedy "replicate argmax(w/cnt)" loop, use a fully vectorized water-filling solver: a     │
│                          │ batched binary search over the load threshold t, where each logical expert i gets         │
│                          │ ceil(w_i/t) replicas (clamped to >=1), followed by an exact residual fixup that hands     │
│                          │ leftover replica slots to the experts with the largest w/cnt. This directly minimizes the │
│                          │ maximum per-replica load w_i/cnt_i (the quantity that drives expert balancedness), and    │
│                          │ the physical layout is then built with a single batched `searchsorted` instead of r       │
│                          │ sequential scatter steps. (2) GPU packing: LPT greedy for the initial assignment, then a  │
│                          │ stronger pairwise-swap local search that, for the most-loaded GPU, evaluates swaps        │
│                          │ against *every* other GPU (not just the least-loaded one), choosing the item/partner pair │
│                          │ that minimizes the resulting maximum load. Packing cardinality is preserved by swaps, and │
│                          │ loads are updated incrementally so each round is O(P·k) vectorized ops. Both stages are   │
│                          │ batched across all layers/nodes, giving both better balance and much lower runtime than   │
│                          │ the per-item Python loops of the reference implementation.                                │
