╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ lpt_replicate_experts                                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the naive `weight/logcnt` argmax heuristic in `replicate_experts` with a proper   │
│                          │ LPT-style greedy over post-replication loads. Currently the heuristic keeps duplicating   │
│                          │ the hottest expert even when its per-replica load `w/c` has already dropped, wasting      │
│                          │ redundancy budget. Instead, at each step we evaluate the *resulting* per-replica load     │
│                          │ `w/(c+1)` for every logical expert (i.e., what its load would become if we gave it one    │
│                          │ more replica) and pick the expert with the highest resulting load — which is exactly the  │
│                          │ replica placement that minimizes the current maximum per-replica load across all          │
│                          │ replicas. This mirrors the proven LPT `balanced_packing` structure (iterative             │
│                          │ argmax/argmin over candidate loads) and directly targets the weakest metric, expert       │
│                          │ balancedness (0.25). The loop is over the (small) number of redundant experts only, and   │
│                          │ each iteration is a single batched op across all layers, so runtime impact is negligible  │
│                          │ compared to the existing implementation (same loop count, same per-iteration cost).       │
